Description: Quantifies runoff, soil loss and nutrient loss with their
    uncertainty and turns them into conservation decisions. Formula-based
    tools include runoff by the curve number method, peak flow by the
    rational method, soil loss by the Revised Universal Soil Loss Equation
    with its factors (slope length and steepness from terrain, cover from the
    normalised difference vegetation index, erodibility from soil texture,
    and a rainfall erosivity index), storm sediment yield by the Modified
    Universal Soil Loss Equation, nutrient loss by mass balance with
    enrichment ratios, empirical nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium loss
    equations from Indian runoff-plot experiments, and flow routing of loads
    to watershed outlets, with sink filling of elevation models,
    gully-risk terrain indices and validation statistics for runoff-plot
    data. A raster pipeline runs the soil-loss chain for any
    region on projected or geographic grids, with curve-number, soil-texture,
    hydrologic soil group and support-practice look-up tables for global
    use, and Indian standards: particle-size conversion from the
    International and Indian Standard systems, Soil Health Card ratings,
    erosivity from annual rainfall with regional relations, slope classes and erosion classes. Any input can be uncertain, and the predictive
    distribution of any spatial model, from kriging to artificial
    intelligence (AI) models such as quantile regression forests, deep
    ensembles and conformal predictors, can be used in place of a formula.
    Decision tools then map where soil loss exceeds a tolerable limit or
    nutrient loss exceeds a target, with expected-loss, regret and
    break-even cost-ratio maps; value-of-information maps, in closed form or
    by an ensemble Kalman update for non-Gaussian AI ensembles; greedy
    budget-constrained designs for new runoff plots; decision-based
    comparison of competing models; and scale operators from pixels to
    districts and watersheds. Machine-learning predictive quantiles are
    joined across space with a spatial Gaussian copula. Also includes a
    dependency-free kriging engine and plain-language summaries of decision
    maps. Methods follow decision-theoretic value of information (Raiffa and
    Schlaifer, 1961, "Applied Statistical Decision Theory"), threshold-based
    elementary scores (Ehm et al., 2016, <doi:10.1111/rssb.12154>), quantile
    regression forests (Meinshausen, 2006,
    <https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v7/meinshausen06a.html>) and the ensemble
    Kalman filter (Evensen, 1994, <doi:10.1029/94JC00572>). The name
    'TerravAI' stands for Threshold Expected-Regret and Risk Analysis with
    Value of information and Artificial Intelligence.
